@node CMacs Roamgraph
@chapter CMacs Roamgraph: the org-roam knowledge graph, natively

@kbd{M-x cmacs-roamgraph} opens your org-roam link graph as a live
libregnum scene inside a cmacs buffer.  It is the in-editor replacement
for @code{org-roam-ui}, which runs a local web server and drives a
browser.

Everything happens inside the editor: orbit with the mouse, click a note
to select it, navigate by keyboard, search incrementally, press
@key{RET} to open the note at exactly the heading it lives under.  It
renders identically under pgtk and under @command{emacs --lrg}.

Requires @option{--with-cmacs-roamgraph} (part of the standard flag
set), which in turn needs @option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} and
@option{--with-cmacs-glib}.

@menu
* Roamgraph Entry Points::      Opening a graph.
* Roamgraph Navigation::        The two keyboard tiers.
* Roamgraph Search::            Finding a note.
* Roamgraph Panes::             The node list, inspector and tags.
* Roamgraph Filtering::         Narrowing the graph, and colour schemes.
* Roamgraph Appearance::        Colours, sizes and labels.
* Roamgraph Data Sources::      Where the nodes and edges come from.
* Roamgraph Layout::            How the force solver works.
* Roamgraph Architecture::      The three translation-unit classes.
@end menu

@node Roamgraph Entry Points
@section Entry points

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph
The whole graph, laid out flat.
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph-3d
The whole graph in three dimensions.
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph-here
Centred on the org-roam node at point.  The daily driver: you are
reading a note and want to see what it sits next to.
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph-local
The N-hop neighbourhood of the node at point; a prefix argument sets N.
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph-jump
Jump to a note by name through @code{completing-read}.
@item M-x cmacs-roamgraph-refresh
Re-read the source and rebuild.
@end table

@node Roamgraph Navigation
@section Navigation

An org-roam graph is a general directed graph with cycles, not a tree,
so ``up'' and ``down'' have to be defined.  Rather than force one
meaning onto @kbd{hjkl}, roamgraph offers two tiers, both always live.

@subsection Spatial: @kbd{h} @kbd{j} @kbd{k} @kbd{l}

Selects the nearest node in that direction @emph{on screen}, within a
45-degree cone.  This is what you mean when you are looking at a picture
of a graph and want the node over there.  It is defined everywhere, has
no dead ends, and ignores link structure entirely.

Candidates are scored by @math{distance / cos^3(angle)}, so alignment
matters more than raw distance; ties break on the lower node id, so
repeated presses never oscillate between two equally good candidates.

A cone metric is not self-inverse, so roamgraph keeps a directional
trail and @emph{replays} the reverse move rather than recomputing it:
@kbd{l} then @kbd{h} always returns exactly where you started.  The
trail is cleared whenever the camera moves, since the projection it was
computed against no longer holds.

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-follow-camera
The camera deliberately does not follow every move.
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-follow-camera} defaults to @code{edge}: it moves
only when the target would otherwise be off-screen.  Flying on every
keypress would invalidate the very projection the spatial keys navigate
by, so the next @kbd{l} would mean something different.

@subsection Topological: @kbd{]} @kbd{[} @kbd{<} @kbd{>} @kbd{o}

@table @kbd
@item ]
Descend a forward link.
@item [
Ascend: pop the breadcrumb, else follow a backlink.
@item > @r{(or} M-n @r{)}
Next peer, wrapping.
@item < @r{(or} M-p @r{)}
Previous peer, wrapping.
@item o
Choose any of this node's links, forward or back, by name.
@end table

Navigation state is an explicit breadcrumb stack, not a spanning tree
recomputed on every move.  A recomputed tree costs O(V+E) per keypress
and makes the same @kbd{[} mean different things depending on where the
traversal started.

The @dfn{peer set} is the other children of your navigation parent: the
node you arrived from if the last move was an ascent, otherwise your
first backlink.  Peers are ordered by
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-peer-sort} (title by default), which is what makes
@kbd{>} then @kbd{<} return you where you started.

On a hub with forty forward links, cycling is hopeless; @kbd{o} opens
them all in @code{completing-read} instead.

Every move echoes what happened, for example
@samp{@result{} Asahi   (3 back / 7 fwd / peer 1 of 3)}.  Without that
line, graph navigation feels random.

@subsection Reading and rooting

@table @kbd
@item @key{RET}
Open the note.  Honours heading-level nodes: it jumps to the node's own
position and unfolds it, rather than dropping you at line 1 of a long
file.
@item O
Open in another window.
@item y
Copy an @samp{[[id:@dots{}]]} link to the note.
@item g r
Re-root the view on the selection.
@item u@r{, }^
Undo the last re-rooting.  (Both are rebound here: in the parent
libregnum mode they walk the @emph{filesystem}.)
@end table

@subsection View and camera

@table @kbd
@item 2@r{, }3@r{, }v
Flat, three-dimensional, toggle.
@item 0@r{, }@key{home}
Frame the whole graph.
@item f
Ease the camera onto the selection.
@item +@r{, }-
Zoom.
@item w a s d
Move the view around.
@item .
Pin the selection so the solver leaves it alone.
@end table

@kbd{w} @kbd{a} @kbd{s} @kbd{d} slide the view, and a right-drag does
the same with the mouse; both work identically in 2D and 3D, moving the
camera across its own view plane.  Left-drag orbits (in 3D), scroll
zooms, and a right-@emph{click} without movement opens a context menu
--- only a drag moves the view.

That menu routes through @code{cmacs-libregnum-popup-menu}, so it is a
native GTK menu under pgtk and the in-engine libregnum popup under
@command{emacs --lrg}, the same path gnuseye, imgedit and vidstudio
take.  It adapts to the target: on a note, open / open in other window /
copy id link, follow-a-link with the out and in counts, centre here,
show only this neighbourhood, pin, inspect, show in the node list, and a
filter entry for each tag the note carries; on empty space, the view
actions instead.  Anything registered with
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-register-inspector-action} is appended.

The popup is re-scheduled onto the command loop rather than opened
inline: the dispatch runs inside the pselect wait, where a nested GTK
menu loop would re-enter the event machinery.

Scrolling up moves the camera closer, as in every map and browser.  That
is an opt-in too: GDK reports a positive delta for scrolling @emph{down}
and libregnum's zoom kernel moves closer for a positive amount, so
passing the delta straight through --- which the other libregnum scenes
still do --- makes the wheel work backwards.

That is the @emph{map} navigation profile.  libregnum's default is the
CAD profile, where either button orbits and panning is on the middle
button; roamgraph opts out, because a graph is more like a map than a
modelling viewport and because plenty of pointing devices have no
usable middle button.  Other libregnum scenes keep the CAD profile.

The 2D
view is a front-facing camera over the XY plane, with the layout
re-solved and @math{z} pinned to zero; orbiting is disabled while flat
(pan and zoom still work), since tumbling a planar layout only reveals
that everything is coplanar.

It is a @emph{perspective} camera locked head-on, not an orthographic
one.  raylib overloads @code{fovy} to mean the view volume's world
height under an orthographic projection, while graylib's
@code{grl_camera3d_set_fovy} asserts @code{fovy < 180} --- a constraint
that only makes sense for an angle.  Framing a graph wider than 180
world units therefore trips that assertion on every frame, and
@code{g_return_if_fail} leaves the camera holding a stale value.
Head-on perspective keeps @code{fovy} a real angle, and makes zoom (a
dolly) actually do something: under an orthographic projection, moving
the camera changes nothing at all.

@node Roamgraph Search
@section Search

Two commands, because finding things in a graph is two different tasks.

@kbd{/} searches incrementally and highlights @emph{every} match at
once: hits take an accent colour and are labelled unconditionally, and
the rest of the graph dims.  Edges with neither endpoint matching fade
further.  This is the point of a visualiser --- a completion list cannot
show you that all six matches sit in one cluster.  @kbd{C-n} and
@kbd{C-p} walk the hits while you are still typing; @key{RET} keeps the
highlight so @kbd{n} and @kbd{N} stay live in the viewport; @kbd{C-g}
clears it and restores the previous selection.

@kbd{g /} is a @code{completing-read} palette over every note, so
vertico/orderless/marginalia apply.

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-search-style
Matching runs over title, aliases, tags and file name, precomputed and
lowercased once per rebuild.  @code{cmacs-roamgraph-search-style}
selects @code{literal} (the default), @code{regexp} or
@code{orderless}.

Search only highlights; it never re-lays-out the graph.  Rebuilding on
every keystroke would reshuffle every position while you are still
typing.

@node Roamgraph Panes
@section Panes

@kbd{D} lays out the full dashboard: the tag and node lists on the left,
the viewport in the middle, the inspector on the right.  @kbd{W} closes
them again.  Each pane can also be summoned alone: @kbd{e} for the node
list, @kbd{i} for the inspector, @kbd{T} for the tags.

The node list is a sortable table of title, tags, out-degree, in-degree
and PARA group.  The tag pane lists every tag with a count; @key{RET}
filters by it.

The inspector shows everything known about the selected note, and its
forward links are numbered @kbd{1}--@kbd{9} with backlinks lettered
@kbd{a}--@kbd{z}.  Those are live keys: pressing one moves the graph
selection there.  With @kbd{o} in the viewport that gives two ways at a
forty-link hub --- by name, or by position.  It also shows a fontified
preview of the body, skipping the heading and property drawer, and
refreshes on an idle timer because rendering it reads the file: holding
@kbd{j} down must not mean reading forty files.

@code{cmacs-roamgraph-register-inspector-action} adds your own actions.
It installs the Evil binding too, without which a registered @kbd{a}
would just be @code{evil-append} under Doom.

@node Roamgraph Filtering
@section Filtering and colour

@table @kbd
@item t
Filter by tag; the chosen tags are ANDed.
@item c
Clear every filter and the search highlight.
@item M-/
Keep only the search matches and their neighbours.
@item z
Show or hide the unlinked notes.
@item C
Cycle the colour scheme.
@end table

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-show-orphans
Unlinked notes are hidden by default.  One carries no graph information,
and a mature tree has enough of them to ring everything that does.

@kbd{C} cycles four schemes, named in the mode line whenever the default
is not in force: @code{para} (the PARA bucket, a fixed palette so the
colours mean the same thing every session), @code{degree} (cool to warm
by link count), @code{tag} (hue hashed from the first tag) and
@code{recency} (cool to warm by modification time).

Filtering genuinely re-solves the layout, because it changes which notes
exist --- which is exactly why it is separate from search, which only
highlights.

@node Roamgraph Appearance
@section Appearance

Nodes are spheres whose radius grows with the logarithm of their degree,
so hubs read as bigger without dwarfing the map.  Colour is the PARA
bucket: amber for @file{00_inbox}, blue for @file{01_projects}, green
for @file{02_areas}, violet for @file{02_areas/dailies}, yellow for
@file{03_resources}, grey for @file{04_archives}.

Dailies get their own colour rather than inheriting the areas one: they
are a large fraction of a mature notes tree and would otherwise swamp
it.  The palette is fixed rather than hashed from the directory name, so
the colours mean the same thing in every session --- the only way they
become readable at a glance.

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-hub-degree
@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-neighbour-labels
Nodes with at least @code{cmacs-roamgraph-hub-degree} links (12) are
labelled permanently and act as landmarks; everything else labels on
hover, plus the selection and its best-connected neighbours up to
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-neighbour-labels} (8).  That cap matters: a hub can
have a hundred links, and naming all of them buries the map.

Edges are tinted with the average of the two notes they join, muted
toward grey, so a link inside one PARA bucket reads as that bucket's
colour and a cross-bucket link reads as neutral.

Labels sit on a translucent plate, since the geometry would otherwise
run through the text.  The selection gets a wireframe halo in the scene
plus a two-ring screen-space marker; the hovered node and search hits
get thinner rings.  Those rings are screen-space on purpose --- they
read the same at any zoom, which a world-space marker does not.

@kbd{?} opens a legend describing the active colour scheme and the keys.

@node Roamgraph Data Sources
@section Data sources

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-source
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-source} selects a backend; @code{auto} (the
default) picks the first that works.

The @code{roam-db} backend reads
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-db-location} directly through Emacs's builtin
SQLite.  Deliberately @emph{not} through @code{org-roam-db-query}: that
needs both the org-roam package and emacsql loaded, and a native
subsystem should not depend on a package manager having installed
something.  The database is authoritative when it exists --- it already
knows about aliases, refs, tags and heading-level nodes --- and
reimplementing org's ID semantics in C would only drift from the one
implementation Emacs already owns.

Only @code{id} links become edges.  A live notes tree also carries
thousands of @code{https}, @code{file} and @code{fuzzy} links whose
endpoints are not notes at all; including them would produce a hairball
of dangling edges.

@quotation Important
emacsql stores every value @emph{printed}, so text columns come back
wrapped in literal double quotes and @code{properties}/@code{olp} are
printed sexps.  Nothing may be read out of that database without
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-db--unwrap}.  This is the single most likely thing
to get wrong when extending the reader.
@end quotation

@code{cmacs-roamgraph-sources} is an alist of a symbol to a thunk
returning @code{(:nodes VECTOR :edges VECTOR)}; add an entry to teach
roamgraph a new place to get a graph from.  Node plists carry
@code{:id :title :file :level :pos :tags :aliases :refs :group :color},
and any extra keys you add are preserved and handed back by
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-node-at}.

@node Roamgraph Layout
@section Layout

Fruchterman--Reingold with Barnes--Hut repulsion, written in C.

Barnes--Hut is not a deferred optimisation: brute force at ~1200 nodes
is about 1.4M pair evaluations per iteration, tolerable once but
hopeless at 30 FPS.  With the octree, a full solve of a 1166-node graph
takes about 0.7 seconds at 1.7 ms per iteration.  Setting the opening
angle to zero degenerates the approximation into an exact all-pairs sum,
which the test suite uses as a free reference implementation.

Choices worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The ideal edge length is a @emph{constant}, not the textbook
@math{k = C sqrt(area/n)}.  That formula exists to pack a graph into a
fixed frame; here the camera fits itself to the content, so making
@math{k} depend on @math{n} would make the map's scale depend on how
many notes you happen to have.
@item
Gravity scales with the node count @emph{and} with degree.  Fixed
gravity lets the cloud grow without bound as the graph grows; weighting
by degree seats hubs centrally and lets their leaves fan out.  Uniform
gravity balances at one radius, so every node drifts to the same shell
and the middle hollows out.
@item
Components are seeded apart before iterating, so unlinked notes spread
out instead of piling up at the origin.
@item
The solve is stepped and then stops.  A multi-second freeze on
@kbd{M-x} is the org-roam-ui experience being replaced, but a
permanently hot render clock afterwards is pure waste.
@item
A refresh keeps surviving nodes where they are; new nodes are seeded at
the centroid of their placed neighbours.  A rebuild that teleported
everything would destroy the graph's value as a spatial memory aid.
@item
It is deterministic: the solver draws from a per-graph xorshift stream,
never @code{g_random_*}.
@end itemize

@vindex cmacs-roamgraph-max-nodes
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-max-nodes} (4000) caps how much is handed to the
renderer.  Above it the best-connected nodes are kept and a message says
how many were dropped --- silently truncating would read as ``this is
your whole graph'' when it is not.

@node Roamgraph Architecture
@section Architecture

Three translation-unit classes, not the usual two:

@table @file
@item cmacs-roamgraph-scene.c
Includes @file{libregnum.h}, so it may not include @file{lisp.h} ---
raylib's @code{Color} clashes with pgtk's.
@item cmacs-roamgraph-defuns.c
Includes @file{lisp.h} only; the Elisp boundary.
@item cmacs/graphcore/cmacs-graphcore-graph.c
@itemx cmacs/graphcore/cmacs-graphcore-layout.c
Include @emph{neither}, only glib.
@end table

That third class is the point: the graph model and the force solver can
be exercised with no Lisp VM and no GL context, which is why the layout
has real unit tests rather than only screenshots.  Same split as
gnuseye's geodesy (@pxref{CMacs Gnuseye}).

The graph model and the solver are @emph{shared}, not roamgraph's own.
They live in @ref{CMacs Graphcore} and are linked by every graph
visualiser; run their suite with @command{just test-graphcore}, which
needs no display and no built emacs.  roamgraph inherits whatever lands
there --- the closed-form layouts (circle, hex, rings), position
tweening and hierarchical collapse all arrived that way.

C owns geometry, the solver, the pick ray and the node table.  Elisp
owns where the data comes from, what a click means, and every pane.  C
never touches org-roam.  The whole contract is one DEFUN,
@code{cmacs-roamgraph-set-graph}.

@subsection Identity: ids, not indices

libregnum scene node ids are insertion indices and churn on @emph{every}
rebuild.  So every piece of state --- selection, breadcrumbs, matches,
pins --- is keyed on the org-roam id string, and that id is what the C
side stores as the node's @code{path}.  That is what arrives
synchronously in the click dispatch; the numeric id in the same callback
may already be stale by the time it is delivered.

@subsection In-scene labels

Node labels used to be drawn in cairo over the blitted framebuffer,
which happens only under pgtk --- so under @command{emacs --lrg} there
were no labels at all, and they never appeared in a snapshot.  roamgraph
turns on @code{cmacs-libregnum-set-inscene-labels}, which moves the
label pass @emph{into} the framebuffer, where both display backends and
the snapshot path see it.  The cairo pass suppresses itself so nothing
is drawn twice.  @xref{CMacs Libregnum}, for the two rules that pass
must obey.
